orm{\mathbf{u}}_2^2 = \Tr(\mathbf{C}_N). \] Indeed, \[ \E
orm{\mathbf{u}}_2^2 = \E[\mathbf{u}^{	op}\mathbf{u}] = \E[\Tr(\mathbf{u}\mathbf{u}^{	op})] = \Tr(\E[\mathbf{u}\mathbf{u}^{	op}]) = \Tr(\mathbf{C}_N). \] (Divergence under refinement is not a quirk of our toy problem. The canonical example is Lassas and Siltanen’s proof that the total-variation prior loses its edge-preserving character as \(N	o\infty\) [LS04] ; the cure, demanding discretization invariance from the start, was developed in [LSS09] and is the heart of the function-space program [Stu10] that the rest of these notes follow.) For a non-centred Gaussian \[ \mathbf{u} \sim \N( ar{\mathbf{u}},\mathbf{C}_N), \] we have \[ \E
orm{\mathbf{u}}_2^2 = 
orm{ ar{\mathbf{u}}}_2^2 + \Tr(\mathbf{C}_N). \] Thus, the trace measures the total variance of the Gaussian cloud. Why \(\mathbf{C}_N = \sigma^2 \mathbf{I}_N\) is dangerous For the naive coefficient prior, \(\mathbf{C}_N = \sigma^2 \mathbf{I}_N\). Therefore, \[ \Tr(\mathbf{C}_N) = \Tr(\sigma^2 \mathbf{I}_N) = \sigma^2\Tr(\mathbf{I}_N) = \sigma^2 N. \] So the prior expected coefficient energy is \[ \E
orm{\mathbf{u}}_2^2 = \sigma^2 N. \] As \(N\) grows, this quantity grows linearly. The posterior improves this only in directions seen by the data. Since the data space has dimension \(N_d\), the data can remove variance from at most \(N_d\) independent directions. At least \(N-N_d\) directions keep their prior variance. Therefore, \[ \Tr(\mathbf{C}_N^{\obs}) \geq \sigma^2(N-N_d). \] The limit is unbounded Taking the refinement limit, \(N 	o \infty\), with \(N_d\) fixed, we obtain \[ \Tr(\mathbf{C}_N^{\obs}) 	o \infty. \] In particular, \[ \E_{\post_N} 
orm{\mathbf{u}}_2^2 = 
orm{	ilde{\mathbf{u}}_N^{\obs}}_2^2 + \Tr(\mathbf{C}_N^{\obs}) 	o \infty, \] provided the posterior mean does not cancel this behaviour in some artificial way. Since the covariance term alone diverges, the posterior spread is already unbounded. This is the mathematical version of what the samples showed visually. Proposition Let \(\Gb\in\R^{N_d	imes N}\) and let the prior covariance be \(\mathbf{C}_N=\sigma^2 \mathbf{I}_N\), with \(\sigma>0\). Assume the observational noise covariance \(\Cd\) is symmetric positive definite. Then the posterior covariance \[ \mathbf{C}_N^{\obs} = \sigma^2 \mathbf{I}_N - \sigma^4 \Gb^{\T} \left( \sigma^2 \Gb\Gb^{\T} + \Cd ight)^{-1} \Gb \] satisfies \[ \Tr(\mathbf{C}_N^{\obs}) \geq \sigma^2(N-N_d). \] Hence, if \(N_d\) is fixed and \(N	o\infty\), then \[ \Tr(\mathbf{C}_N^{\obs})	o\infty. \] Since \(\operatorname{rank}(\Gb)\leq N_d\), the nullspace of \(\Gb\) has dimension at least \(N-N_d\). Let \[ \{\mathbf{v}_1,\ldots,\mathbf{v}_{N-r}\} \] be an orthonormal basis for \(\operatorname{null}(\Gb)\), where \(r=\operatorname{rank}(\Gb)\). For each such vector, \(\Gb\mathbf{v}_j = 0\). Therefore, \[ \mathbf{C}_N^{\obs}\mathbf{v}_j = \sigma^2\mathbf{v}_j. \] Thus \(\mathbf{C}_N^{\obs}\) has at least \(N-r\) eigenvalues equal to \(\sigma^2\). Since \(r\leq N_d\), it has at least \(N-N_d\) such eigenvalues. Hence \[ \Tr(\mathbf{C}_N^{\obs}) \geq \sigma^2(N-N_d). \] The core mistake The problem is not that Gaussian priors are bad. The problem is not that finite elements are bad. The problem is not even that the posterior mean was computed incorrectly. The problem is that we chose the prior after discretization, directly on the coefficient vector, without asking whether the resulting sequence of priors has a meaningful function-space limit. Warning The prior was not chosen as a probability measure on a function space. It was chosen as a sequence of unrelated finite-dimensional matrices. A matrix \(\mathbf{C}_N \in \R^{N	imes N}\) can be a perfectly valid covariance matrix in \(\R^N\) for every fixed \(N\). But this does not mean that the sequence \(C_1,C_2,C_3,\ldots\) defines a valid covariance operator on a function space. The continuous question is not merely: \[ 	ext{Is } \mathbf{C}_N 	ext{ positive definite for each } N? \] The continuous question is: \[ 	ext{Do these finite-dimensional distributions approximate a coherent probability measure on functions?} \] For the naive diagonal prior with fixed variance per coefficient, the answer is no. What this means philosophically The discretized problem replaced the original problem At the beginning, we pretended to have a continuous inverse problem. But we did not fully define it. We did not specify the model space. We did not specify a prior measure on that space. We did not specify the geometry needed to define the correct adjoint. Instead, we discretized first and formulated the Bayesian problem on the coefficient vector: \[ \mathbf{u} \in \R^N. \] So the actual problem we solved was not the continuous inverse problem. It was a finite-dimensional problem created by the discretization. The mesh did not approximate the model. The mesh became the model. Refinement did not improve the approximation In a healthy numerical method, refinement means that the discrete solution gets closer to the solution of the original problem. But here, refinement changes the probabilistic model itself. For each \(N\), we choose \[ \mathbf{u}_N \sim \N(0,\sigma^2 \mathbf{I}_N). \] When \(N\) increases, this is not a better approximation of the same prior. It is a different prior on a different space, with more independent random coefficients. Thus, refinement does not only increase resolution. It also injects additional prior variance. Refinement changed the problem The posterior for \(N=10\) and the posterior for \(N=50\) are not merely two approximations of the same infinite-dimensional posterior. Under the naive construction, they are posteriors for two different Bayesian models. The notation \(\post_N\) should therefore make us nervous. It is not automatically a convergent sequence. It is only a sequence of probability measures produced by a sequence of finite-dimensional choices. To know whether \(\post_N 	o \post\) for some meaningful function-space posterior \(\post\), we must first define \(\post\). That requires choosing the model space, data space, forward map, noise model, and prior measure before discretizing. Lesson A discretization is not innocent. If the continuous problem has not been defined, the discretization becomes the model. The moral of Act II The naive posterior did not fail because the algebra was wrong. The algebra was perfectly competent. That is what makes the failure instructive. The failure came from asking finite-dimensional linear algebra to make infinite-dimensional modelling choices on our behalf. We started with a function but placed the prior on coefficients. We refined the mesh but did not refine an approximation to a fixed prior measure. We used the posterior mean as evidence of success while ignoring the posterior samples. Once we sampled, the hidden degrees of freedom marched out carrying little banners that read: \[ 	ext{You forgot the function space.} \] Checkpoint The next act begins by stepping back from the computation. Before we choose a basis, before we build a matrix, and before we sample anything, we must ask: what is the mathematical problem we are actually trying to solve? References Matti Lassas and Samuli Siltanen (2004). “Can one use total variation prior for edge-preserving Bayesian inversion?” Inverse Problems 20(5), 1537–1563. The classic proof that a discretize-first prior can degenerate under refinement (total variation). doi:10.1088/0266-5611/20/5/013 Matti Lassas, Eero Saksman, and Samuli Siltanen (2009). “Discretization-invariant Bayesian inversion and Besov space priors.” Inverse Problems and Imaging 3(1), 87–122. Discretization invariance named, formalized, and repaired with Besov priors. doi:10.3934/ipi.2009.3.87 Andrew M. Stuart (2010). “Inverse problems: A Bayesian perspective.” Acta Numerica 19, 451–559. The function-space program that makes refinement safe by construction. doi:10.1017/S0962492910000061 · free PDF (Cambridge Core) ← Act I: Familiar Problem Act III: What Problem? →

Act III: What Problem Are We Actually Solving? Before choosing basis functions or matrices, we must define the mathematical objects whose relationship we want to infer. Part of Think First, Discretize Later . « Overview 1 Familiar Problem 2 Something Is Wrong 3 What Problem? 4 Functional Analysis 5 Data & Adjoint 6 The Prior 7 The Repair 8 The Verdict S Summary Stepping Back The real question The previous act ended with an unpleasant discovery. The naive posterior did not fail because the Gaussian conditioning formula was wrong. It failed because we had never properly said what problem the discretization was supposed to approximate. We began with a function, replaced it by coefficients, put a Gaussian prior on those coefficients, and hoped that mesh refinement would behave like mathematical progress. It did not. The posterior mean looked acceptable, but the posterior measure itself became increasingly unreasonable. So now we pause. Before choosing basis functions, matrices, quadrature rules, or numerical solvers, we ask a more basic question: \[ 	ext{What is the inverse problem?} \] This question is not a request for code. It is a request for modelling choices. The unknown must live somewhere. The data must live somewhere. The forward map must connect those two places. The noise model must describe observational uncertainty. The prior must be a probability measure on the unknown itself, not merely a convenient matrix attached to a discretization. Lesson A Bayesian inverse problem is not born when we assemble a matrix. It is born when we specify the mathematical objects whose relationship we want to infer. F3a, Where does everything live? The model \(m\) lives in a function space \(\M\). The data \(\obs\) live in a finite-dimensional space \(\D=\R^{N_d}\). The forward map \(G:\M	o\D\) connects them. The prior \(\prior\) is a measure on \(\M\), not a matrix. The noise \(
oise\sim\N(0,\Cd)\) lives in \(\D\). What is the unknown object? The first question is deceptively simple: \[ 	ext{What is } m? \] In the toy example, \(m\) is a function on a physical domain \[ \Omega = [0,L]. \] But saying that \(m\) is a function is not enough. There are many kinds of functions. Some are continuous, some are merely square-integrable, some have weak derivatives, some satisfy boundary conditions, and some are so irregular that pointwise values are not even meaningful. Thus, we must decide what class of objects we are willing to regard as possible models. For example, we might choose \[ m \in L^2(\Omega), \] if we only want square-integrability. Or we might choose \[ m \in H^1(\Omega), \] if we want one weak derivative to be square-integrable. Or we might choose a more regular Sobolev space if smoothness is part of our prior information. At this stage, the precise choice is not yet the point. The point is that there must be a choice. Warning The unknown object is not a vector of coefficients. Coefficients are only a coordinate representation after a basis has been chosen. The model space determines what counts as an admissible model, what convergence means, what it means for two models to be close, and what kinds of uncertainty can be placed on the model. What kind of data do we observe? The second question is: \(	ext{What is } \obs?\) In the toy problem, the data are finitely many real numbers: \[ \obs =  egin{pmatrix} [\obs]_1 \ \vdots \ [\obs]_{N_d} \end{pmatrix} \in \R^{N_d}. \] Thus, a natural data space is \[ \D = \R^{N_d}. \] This looks simple, and in many ways it is. Finite-dimensional spaces are mathematically friendly. All norms on \(\R^{N_d}\) induce the same topology, linear maps into and out of \(\R^{N_d}\) are easier to control, and completeness is automatic. But even in finite dimensions, the geometry matters. The data covariance \(\Cd\) determines how residuals are weighted. The quantity \[ \obs - G m \] is not merely a vector of errors. It is a vector of errors measured relative to the uncertainty in the observations. A natural data misfit is therefore \[  rac{1}{2} 
orm{\obs-G m}_{\Cd^{-1}}^2 =  rac{1}{2} (\obs-G m)^{	op} \Cd^{-1}(\obs-G m). \] Intuition The data space tells us what observations are. The noise covariance tells us how loudly each component of the data is allowed to complain. What is the forward map? The third question is: \(	ext{How does } m 	ext{ produce data?}\) This is the role of the forward map \[ G : \M 	o \D. \] In the toy example, \[ [G(m)]_i = \int_{\Omega} K_i(x)\,m(x)\,dx, \qquad i=1,\ldots,N_d. \] The forward map is the mathematical description of the experiment. It tells us which features of the model are visible to the data. If the forward map is smoothing, averaging, or compact, then the inverse problem will usually be ill-posed in a deterministic sense. Small changes in data may correspond to large changes in some model directions. Before discretizing, we must know what kind of map \(G\) is. Is it linear? Is it continuous? Is it bounded between the chosen model and data spaces? Does it make sense for every \(m\in\M\)? For a linear inverse problem, we require at least \[ G(\alpha m_1+ eta m_2) = \alpha G m_1 +  eta G m_2. \] For a well-behaved function-space formulation, we usually also want \(G\) to be continuous: \[ m_n 	o m 	ext{ in } \M \quad \Longrightarrow \quad G m_n 	o G m 	ext{ in } \D. \] Warning A matrix is not the forward operator. A matrix is a representation of the forward operator after choosing coordinates in the model and data spaces. What does prior information mean? The fourth question is: \(	ext{What do we know before observing the data?}\) Prior information may include expected amplitude, smoothness, length scale, boundary behaviour, correlations, sign constraints, or physical plausibility. In a Bayesian formulation, this information is encoded by a prior probability measure \(\prior\) on the model space \(\M\). Thus, instead of placing a Gaussian directly on coefficients, we write \[ m \sim \prior. \] If the prior is Gaussian, we may write \[ \prior = \N(m_0,C_0), \] where \(m_0\) is the prior mean and \(C_0\) is a covariance operator on the model space. The word operator matters. In function space, covariance is not merely a matrix. It is a mathematical object that must act coherently on functions. It must encode a genuine probability measure on the model space. Lesson Prior information is not a numerical stabilizer sprinkled on top of a matrix problem. It is part of the definition of the inverse problem. The ingredients of a well-posed Bayesian inverse problem We now assemble the pieces. A Bayesian inverse problem should be formulated before discretization by specifying the following ingredients. F3e, Choose your modelling ingredients. Try different combinations. The coherence report checks whether the choices are mathematically compatible. Model space M L²(Ω), square-integrable H¹(Ω), one weak derivative H²(Ω), two weak derivatives Data type Integral averages Point samples Prior covariance Identity (σ²I) Bessel-Sobolev (trace-class) Gaussian kernel Noise level Small (1%) Medium (5%) Large (20%) Select ingredients to see the coherence report. Model space The model space is the mathematical home of the unknown: \[ m \in \M. \] The choice of \(\M\) determines which objects are admissible models. It also determines the meaning of convergence, continuity, size, regularity, and eventually the geometry needed to define adjoints. Typical choices include Hilbert spaces such as \[ L^2(\Omega), \qquad H^1(\Omega), \qquad H^s(\Omega), \] possibly with boundary conditions built into the space. We should choose \(\M\) by asking modelling questions: What kind of object is physically meaningful? How much regularity should the model have? What boundary behaviour is appropriate? What perturbations should count as small? What kind of prior measure can live on this space? Data space The data space is the mathematical home of the observations: \[ \obs \in \D. \] In the toy problem, \(\D = \R^{N_d}\). This finite-dimensional choice is convenient, but it does not remove all modelling decisions. We still need to decide how data residuals are measured. If the observational noise covariance is \(\Cd\), then the natural data norm is \[ 
orm{d}_{\Cd^{-1}}^2 = d^{	op} \Cd^{-1}d. \] This norm weights residuals according to observational uncertainty. A residual in a highly uncertain datum should not be punished as severely as an equal-sized residual in a very accurate datum. Thus, even when \(\D\) is finite-dimensional, the data geometry carries meaning. Forward map The forward map connects models to predicted data: \[ G : \M 	o \D. \] For the linear case, \[ G(\alpha m_1+ eta m_2) = \alpha G m_1+ eta G m_2. \] In the integral-kernel example, \[ [G(m)]_i = \int_\Omega K_i(x)\,m(x)\,dx. \] The forward map must be compatible with the model space. For instance, the integral above must be well-defined for every \(m\in\M\). If the kernels \(K_i\) are square-integrable and \(\M = L^2(\Omega)\), then each datum is well-defined by the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality: \[ \left| \int_\Omega K_i(x)\,m(x)\,dx ight| \leq 
orm{K_i}_{L^2(\Omega)} 
orm{m}_{L^2(\Omega)}. \] This compatibility is exactly the sort of issue that disappears from view if we start with a matrix. Noise model The noise model describes the relationship between the true predicted data and the observed data: \[ 
oise \sim \N(0,\Cd). \] The data equation is \[ \obs = G m + 
oise. \] Equivalently, conditional on \(m\), the data are distributed as \[ \obs \mid m \sim \N(G m,\Cd). \] For finite-dimensional data, this gives the likelihood \[ \pi(\obs\mid m) \propto \exp\left( - rac{1}{2} 
orm{\obs-G m}_{\Cd^{-1}}^2 ight). \] It is useful to define the data-misfit potential \[ \Phi(m;\obs) =  rac{1}{2} 
orm{\obs-G m}_{\Cd^{-1}}^2. \] Then \[ \pi(\obs\mid m) \propto \exp(-\Phi(m;\obs)). \] Prior measure The prior measure describes uncertainty about the model before the data are observed: \[ m \sim \prior. \] This is where the naive construction failed. We did not choose a measure on \(\M\). We chose matrices \[ \mathbf{C}_N=\sigma^2 \mathbf{I}_N \] on coefficient spaces \(\R^N\). Each fixed matrix was a valid covariance matrix in finite dimensions, but the sequence did not define a coherent function-space prior. In the function-space formulation, the prior is chosen directly on \(\M\): \[ \prior \in \mathcal{P}(\M), \] where \(\mathcal{P}(\M)\) denotes the set of probability measures on \(\M\). For a Gaussian prior, we write \[ \prior = \N(m_0,C_0). \] Here \(C_0\) must be a valid covariance operator for a Gaussian measure on the chosen model space. Warning A covariance matrix answers a finite-dimensional question. A covariance operator answers a function-space question. Confusing the two is how the naive posterior slipped through the trapdoor. The posterior measure Once the ingredients are specified, the posterior can be written in a way that does not depend on any particular discretization. For Gaussian observational noise, define \[ \Phi(m;\obs) =  rac{1}{2} 
orm{\obs-G m}_{\Cd^{-1}}^2. \] The posterior measure \(\post\) is then formally given by \[  rac{d\post}{d\prior}(m) =  rac{1}{Z(\obs)} \exp(-\Phi(m;\obs)), \] where \[ Z(\obs) = \int_{\M} \exp(-\Phi(m;\obs)) \,d\prior(m) \] is the normalizing constant. This formula is one of the cleanest ways to write Bayes' theorem in function space. It says that the posterior is obtained by reweighting the prior according to how well each model predicts the observed data. Intuition The prior says which models are possible before the experiment. The likelihood tilts that probability toward models that explain the data. The posterior is the tilted measure. F3c, The posterior as reweighting. The prior (left) assigns higher probability to smoother functions. The posterior (right) contains the same family of functions, none are filtered out, but the weights have shifted. Rougher functions, which fit the data better, now carry more probability mass. The posterior is not a new set of models; it is the prior reweighted by the likelihood. For this expression to define a probability measure, we need \[ 0 < Z(\obs) < \infty. \] If this holds, then \(\post\) is a well-defined probability measure on the same model space \(\M\) as the prior. The order matters We can now state the central rule. Lesson Choose \[ \M,\qquad \D,\qquad G,\qquad \Cd,\qquad \prior \] before choosing a mesh, basis, quadrature rule, or matrix representation. The order matters because each numerical object should approximate a mathematical object that already exists. A mesh should approximate the domain. A finite-dimensional subspace should approximate the model space. A matrix should represent the forward map. A discrete covariance should approximate the covariance operator. A discrete posterior should approximate the function-space posterior. The wrong order is \[ 	ext{basis} \quad\Rightarrow\quad 	ext{coefficients} \quad\Rightarrow\quad 	ext{matrix prior} \quad\Rightarrow\quad 	ext{posterior}. \] The better order is \[ 	ext{model space} \quad\Rightarrow\quad 	ext{prior measure} \quad\Rightarrow\quad 	ext{forward map} \quad\Rightarrow\quad 	ext{posterior} \quad\Rightarrow\quad 	ext{discretization}. \] Checkpoint Discretization should enter as an approximation of a problem that already has a meaning without the discretization. F3b, The wrong order versus the right order. Left: the naive route starts with a mesh and builds upward, but the matrix prior has no continuous object to approximate , the chain is broken. Right: the function-space route formulates the problem first, then discretizes. The discrete posterior approximates a target that already exists. Well-posedness In deterministic inverse problems, well-posedness is often associated with three requirements: existence, uniqueness, and stability. In Bayesian inverse problems, these ideas are modified. We no longer demand that there is a unique model matching the data. Instead, we ask whether the posterior distribution is well-defined, meaningful, and stable. (This whole act is our retelling of a program with a definite address: Stuart’s Acta Numerica survey [Stu10] formulated Bayesian inversion on function spaces and proved the corresponding well-posedness; Dashti and Stuart [DS17] is the mature handbook treatment.) Existence Existence means that the posterior measure exists. Using the Radon-Nikodym form \[  rac{d\post}{d\prior}(m) =  rac{1}{Z(\obs)} \exp(-\Phi(m;\obs)), \] existence requires the normalizing constant \[ Z(\obs) = \int_{\M} \exp(-\Phi(m;\obs)) \,d\prior(m) \] to satisfy \[ 0 < Z(\obs) < \infty. \] The upper bound ensures that the posterior can be normalized. The lower bound ensures that the data are not assigned zero probability under the model in a way that destroys the conditioning. For the linear Gaussian problem with finite-dimensional data, this condition is usually benign when \(G\) is continuous, \(\Cd\) is positive definite, and the prior is a genuine probability measure on \(\M\). Lesson Existence means that Bayes' theorem produces an actual probability measure, not just a decorative formula. Uniqueness in the probabilistic sense In an infinite-dimensional inverse problem, we should not usually expect a unique model \(m\) that explains the data. There may be infinitely many models that fit the observations to within noise. Bayesian uniqueness means something different. It means that, once we have specified \[ \M,\qquad \D,\qquad G,\qquad \Cd,\qquad \prior, \] the posterior measure is uniquely determined. That is, the object of inference is not "the one true coefficient vector," but rather \[ \post = \mathbb{P}(m\in\cdot \mid \obs). \] There may be many plausible models, but there is one posterior distribution describing their plausibility relative to the chosen assumptions. Intuition Bayesian inversion does not always collapse uncertainty into a single answer. It gives uncertainty a precise shape. Stability with respect to data Stability means that small changes in the observed data should produce small changes in the posterior. Suppose we observe two nearby data vectors \(\obs,\obs' \in \D\). They produce two posterior measures \(\mu^{\obs}\) and \(\mu^{\obs'}\). A Bayesian inverse problem is stable if \[ \obs \approx \obs' \quad\Longrightarrow\quad \mu^{\obs} \approx \mu^{\obs'}. \] To make this precise, one chooses a distance between probability measures, such as the Hellinger distance. The exact metric is less important here than the principle: the posterior should not change violently when the data are perturbed slightly. For linear Gaussian inverse problems with finite-dimensional data, stability is often obtained when the forward map is continuous and the likelihood potential depends continuously on the data. Warning A stable posterior does not mean the inverse problem has become magically well-conditioned in the deterministic sense. It means the probability measure responds continuously to perturbations in the data. Convergence under discretization Finally, the discretized posteriors should converge to the function-space posterior. Let \(\M_N \subset \M\) be a sequence of finite-dimensional approximation spaces, and let \(\post_N\) denote the posterior obtained by discretizing the already-formulated function-space problem. A good discretization should satisfy, in an appropriate sense, \[ \post_N 	o \post \qquad 	ext{as} \qquad N	o\infty. \] This is very different from merely defining a new posterior for every \(N\). The limiting posterior \(\post\) must already exist. The discretizations are then judged by whether they approximate it. The naive construction failed precisely here. It produced a sequence \(\post_1,\post_2,\post_3,\ldots\) without first defining a target posterior \(\post\) on function space. Lesson Convergence under discretization is only meaningful after the continuous problem has been posed. F3d, Well-posedness, Bayesian style. Existence: the normalizing constant \(Z(\obs)\) is finite and positive. Uniqueness: the inputs \(\M, \D, G, \Cd, \prior\) determine one posterior measure. Stability: nearby data produce nearby posteriors. Convergence: discrete posteriors \(\post_N\) approach the function-space posterior \(\post\), not drift away from it. The moral of Act III We now know what went wrong in the naive workflow. The order was upside down. We used discretization to define the model space. We used a matrix to define the prior. We used a transpose without first specifying the geometry. Then we expected refinement to behave as if there were a continuous problem underneath. The cure is to reverse the order. First choose the mathematical ingredients: \[ \M,\qquad \D,\qquad G,\qquad \Cd,\qquad \prior. \] Then define the posterior measure: \[  rac{d\post}{d\prior}(m) =  rac{1}{Z(\obs)} \exp\left( - rac{1}{2} 
orm{\obs-G m}_{\Cd^{-1}}^2 ight). \] Only after this do we discretize. Checkpoint The next act is a detour through functional analysis. We need to understand what kinds of spaces are available, what structure they carry, and why Hilbert spaces so often become the natural habitat for linear Bayesian inverse problems. References Andrew M. Stuart (2010). “Inverse problems: A Bayesian perspective.” Acta Numerica 19, 451–559. The manifesto of this act: formulate on function space, discretize last. doi:10.1017/S0962492910000061 · free PDF (Cambridge Core) Masoumeh Dashti and Andrew M. Stuart (2017). “The Bayesian Approach to Inverse Problems.” In Handbook of Uncertainty Quantification , Springer. The mature, detailed development of the same program. doi:10.1007/978-3-319-12385-1_7 ← Act II: Something Is Wrong Act IV: Functional Analysis →

Part 3c: Conditional Expectation Conditioning on σ-algebras, conditional probability, and the resolution of the Borel–Kolmogorov paradox. Part of Bayes, Measure-Theoretically . « Overview 1 The Game 2 High-School Solution 3a Points → Events 3b Conditioning 3c Cond. Expectation 3d Disintegration 3e Bayesian Inversion 3f Back to Finite 4 Summary A word before we begin The next few parts are the most mathematically demanding. We are going to introduce conditional expectation , disintegration , and Bayesian inversion in measure-theoretic language. These concepts take effort to absorb, but they will click into place, trust the process. One stylistic choice: even though our switch problem lives in finite land, we will write definitions using integrals rather than sums. This is deliberate. Integrals are the more general notation: they work in both finite and continuous spaces, and they lead to cleaner derivations. If you want to stay in finite land, just mentally replace every integral by a weighted sum , everything will follow exactly the same. The payoff for battling the infinite case now: by the end of Part 3f , we will take the full measure-theoretic Bayes formula and apply it back to the finite switch problem, bringing everything home to where we started. Conditioning on a σ-algebra: the switch problem In Part 3b , we saw that conditioning on individual events breaks down when the conditioning event has probability zero. The Borel–Kolmogorov paradox showed that different ways of “shrinking” toward a measure-zero set can give different answers. The resolution is to condition on a σ-algebra rather than on a set. To understand what this means, we will use a coarser σ-algebra on the switch problem's model space. A coarser σ-algebra on \(\M\) Recall that the full σ-algebra on the model space is \(\SigmaM=2^{\M}\), every subset is an event. But we can also work with a coarser σ-algebra, one that cannot distinguish between certain outcomes. Define: \[\mathcal{G}=\{\varnothing,\;\{00,01\},\;\{10,11\},\;\M\}.\] This σ-algebra cannot distinguish between: outcomes \(00\) and \(01\) (both in \(\{00,01\}\)), outcomes \(10\) and \(11\) (both in \(\{10,11\}\)). So \(\mathcal{G}\) represents the information: “I only know whether the first switch is off (\(00\) or \(01\)) or on (\(10\) or \(11\)).” A σ-algebra is a collection of distinctions A σ-algebra is not only a collection of events. It is a collection of distinctions we are allowed to make . \(\mathcal{G}\) can ask: “is the first switch off or on?” But it cannot ask: “is the state exactly \(00\)?” because \(\{00\}
otin\mathcal{G}\). The test is simple: \(A\in\mathcal{G}\) if and only if the coarse observer can decide whether \(A\) happened. This will make \(\mathcal{G}\)-measurability feel natural later: a \(\mathcal{G}\)-measurable function is one that never uses more information than \(\mathcal{G}\) allows. F3c-1, A coarser σ-algebra on \(\M\). The full σ-algebra \(\SigmaM=2^{\M}\) can distinguish all four states. The coarser \(\mathcal{G}\) can only tell whether the first switch is on or off. Outcomes within each group are indistinguishable . Conditional expectation: averaging over indistinguishable outcomes Before we condition on a σ-algebra, let us recall what the ordinary expectation \(\mathbb{E}[X]\) does. It averages \(X\) over all outcomes, weighted by their probabilities. It is the answer to the question: If I know nothing, what is my best guess for \(X\)? (“Best” here means best in the mean-square sense: among all constants, \(\mathbb{E}[X]\) minimizes the expected squared error \(\mathbb{E}[(X-c)^2]\). Other loss functions single out other summaries , absolute error is minimized by a median, for instance. Throughout this part, “best guess” is shorthand for the mean-square-optimal predictor.) (Defining conditional expectation relative to a σ-algebra, via the Radon–Nikodym theorem, is Kolmogorov’s construction [Kol33] ; the modern form we follow is in every graduate text, e.g. Kallenberg [Kal21] .) Let \(X:\M	o\mathbb{R}\) be a random variable on the model space. For concreteness, suppose: \[X(00)=0,\quad X(01)=2,\quad X(10)=1,\quad X(11)=3.\] The ordinary expectation is the prior-weighted average over all four outcomes: \[\mathbb{E}[X]=\sum_{m\in\M}\muMzero(\{m\})\,X(m) =0.4\cdot 0+0.1\cdot 2+0.1\cdot 1+0.4\cdot 3=1.5.\] This is a single number. It is the best guess for \(X\) when we have no information at all, when we cannot distinguish any outcome from any other. In σ-algebra language, this is conditioning on the coarsest possible σ-algebra \(\{\varnothing,\M\}\), which cannot distinguish anything. Ordinary expectation is conditional expectation with no information. Expectation as compression If you must replace \(X\) by one number, and you know nothing, then \(\mathbb{E}[X]\) is the value that preserves the average. It is a one-number compression of \(X\), the best you can do when the only visible events are \(\{\varnothing,\M\}\). Now suppose we have partial information. Specifically, suppose we know which group the outcome belongs to in the coarser σ-algebra \(\mathcal{G}=\{\varnothing,\{00,01\},\{10,11\},\M\}\), but we cannot distinguish outcomes within each group. What is our best guess for \(X\) now? The answer is the conditional expectation \(\mathbb{E}[X\mid\mathcal{G}]\). It is no longer a single number, it is a random variable , because the best guess depends on which group we find ourselves in. Ordinary expectation compressed \(X\) to one number because we could not distinguish anything. Conditional expectation compresses \(X\) to one value per visible information cell , because we can distinguish groups but not individuals within them. Two design rules We want a replacement \(Y\) for \(X\) that satisfies two rules: 1. \(Y\) must be \(\mathcal{G}\)-measurable. It can only use the information visible to \(\mathcal{G}\). It cannot secretly distinguish \(00\) from \(01\) if \(\mathcal{G}\) cannot. 2. \(Y\) must preserve all visible totals. On every event the coarse observer can actually see, the total weighted value of \(Y\) must equal that of \(X\). Conditional expectation is the visible replacement of \(X\) that preserves all visible totals. Formally: for an integrable random variable \(X\) (meaning \(\mathbb{E}[|X|] < \infty\), automatic in our finite example, but a genuine requirement in general), the conditional expectation \(\mathbb{E}[X\mid\mathcal{G}]\) is the (essentially unique) \(\mathcal{G}\)-measurable random variable \(Y\) such that: \[\int_H Y\,d\muMzero = \int_H X\,d\muMzero \qquad	ext{for every } H\in\mathcal{G}.\] If you just felt a slight panic at the sight of an integral, don't worry. In our finite setting, an integral against a measure is simply a weighted sum . The condition above just says: \[\sum_{m\in H} Y(m)\,\muMzero(\{m\}) = \sum_{m\in H} X(m)\,\muMzero(\{m\}) \qquad	ext{for every } H\in\mathcal{G}.\] That is: the total weighted value of \(Y\) on each group must equal the total weighted value of \(X\) on that same group. We write it as an integral because the same definition works in continuous spaces, where sums become integrals, but the idea is identical: match the totals on each indistinguishable group . This is a statement about integrals , not about ratios. There is no normalization, no division by \(\muMzero(H)\). The conditional expectation is the \(\mathcal{G}\)-measurable function whose integrals over \(\mathcal{G}\)-sets match those of \(X\). Existence and uniqueness (up to null sets) follow from the Radon–Nikodým theorem. Why no normalization? If the definition involved dividing by \(\muMzero(H)\), we would be back to the same problem: in continuous spaces, \(\muMzero(H)\) can be zero. The integral condition avoids this entirely. It only requires that the total mass of \(Y\) and \(X\) agree on each \(\mathcal{G}\)-set, no division anywhere. Two honest footnotes to this slogan. First, whenever we solve the condition on a positive-mass cell, the division reappears, as it does below. Second, on a cell of measure zero the condition reads \(0=0\): it does not perform an illegal division, but neither does it determine a value there. The definition avoids the crash; it does not conjure information out of nothing. In our finite example, this integral condition is easy to solve. Because \(Y\) must be \(\mathcal{G}\)-measurable, it must be constant on each group. Why? Recall that \(\mathcal{G}\)-measurable means that for every interval \(I\subset\mathbb{R}\), the set of outcomes where \(Y\in I\) must belong to \(\mathcal{G}\). In our case, \(\mathcal{G}=\{\varnothing,\{00,01\},\{10,11\},\M\}\). If \(Y\) took different values on \(00\) and \(01\), say \(Y(00)=a\) and \(Y(01)=b\) with \(a
eq b\), then the preimage of a small interval around \(a\) would contain \(00\) but not \(01\), i.e. \(\{00\}\). But \(\{00\}
otin\mathcal{G}\). So \(Y\) cannot distinguish \(00\) from \(01\); it must assign them the same value. The same argument applies to \(\{10,11\}\). In general, a \(\mathcal{G}\)-measurable function is constant on the atoms (finest indivisible sets) of \(\mathcal{G}\). On \(\{00,01\}\), the condition \(\int_{\{00,01\}} Y\,d\muMzero = \int_{\{00,01\}} X\,d\muMzero\) becomes: \[Y\cdot\muMzero(\{00,01\}) = \muMzero(\{00\})\cdot X(00)+\muMzero(\{01\})\cdot X(01).\] Since \(\muMzero(\{00,01\})=0.5>0\), we can divide here (it is a consequence of the definition, not the definition itself): \[Y ig|_{\{00,01\}}= rac{0.4\cdot 0+0.1\cdot 2}{0.5}=0.4.\] Similarly, on \(\{10,11\}\): \[Y ig|_{\{10,11\}}= rac{0.1\cdot 1+0.4\cdot 3}{0.5}=2.6.\] Therefore: \[\mathbb{E}[X\mid\mathcal{G}](m)= egin{cases}0.4,&m\in\{00,01\},\ 2.6,&m\in\{10,11\}.\end{cases}\] But what if the group has measure zero? A sharp-eyed reader will ask: “You divided by \(\muMzero(\{00,01\})\). What if that were zero? Aren't you back to dividing by nothing?” No, and this is the beauty of the definition. The integral equality is meant to stay an equality . If \(\muMzero(H)=0\) for some \(H\in\mathcal{G}\), then every subset of \(H\) also has measure zero, so the right-hand side \(\sum_{m\in H} X(m)\,\muMzero(\{m\})\) is zero too. The equation reads \(0=0\), which is trivially true for any value of \(Y\) on that group. The conditional expectation is simply not determined on null sets, and it doesn't need to be, because those outcomes never occur. The definition sidesteps division by zero entirely, like a spy ducking under laser beams. The integral condition never asks us to divide; it only asks us to match totals . When the total is zero, there is nothing to match. Notice the progression: \(\mathbb{E}[X]=1.5\) is a single number (no information), while \(\mathbb{E}[X\mid\mathcal{G}]\) is a random variable that takes different values on different groups (partial information). If we conditioned on the full σ-algebra \(\SigmaM\), we would recover \(X\) itself (complete information). Intuition The ordinary expectation \(\mathbb{E}[X]\) averages over all outcomes , the best guess when you know nothing. The conditional expectation \(\mathbb{E}[X\mid\mathcal{G}]\) is defined by an integral condition: it is the \(\mathcal{G}\)-measurable function whose integrals over \(\mathcal{G}\)-sets match those of \(X\). In the discrete case this simplifies to the familiar average within each group, but the definition itself involves no normalization, and that is precisely why it continues to work in continuous spaces where division would fail. From conditional expectation to conditional probability So far \(X\) could be any numerical quantity. But we are interested in probabilities , not arbitrary averages. The bridge is simple: probability is already an expectation in disguise. Given an event \(A\in\SigmaM\), its indicator function \(\mathbf{1}_A:\M	o\{0,1\}\) is a random variable: it equals \(1\) on \(A\) and \(0\) outside. An indicator is a question turned into a random variable : it asks “did \(A\) happen?” and outputs yes (1) or no (0). The expectation of this indicator is just the probability of \(A\): \[\mathbb{E}[\mathbf{1}_A]=\mathbb{P}(A).\] So if expectation of an indicator gives probability, then conditional expectation of an indicator should give conditional probability. We apply the machine to yes/no variables: condition on \(\mathcal{G}\). By the same integral definition, the conditional expectation \(\mathbb{E}[\mathbf{1}_A\mid\mathcal{G}]\) is the \(\mathcal{G}\)-measurable random variable satisfying: \[\int_H \mathbb{E}[\mathbf{1}_A\mid\mathcal{G}]\,d\muMzero = \int_H \mathbf{1}_A\,d\muMzero = \muMzero(A\cap H) \qquad	ext{for every } H\in\mathcal{G}.\] We define the conditional probability of \(A\) given \(\mathcal{G}\) by: \[\mathbb{P}(A\mid\mathcal{G}):=\mathbb{E}[\mathbf{1}_A\mid\mathcal{G}].\] This is the measure-theoretic definition of conditional probability . It is defined by the integral condition \(\int_H \mathbb{P}(A\mid\mathcal{G})\,d\muMzero = \muMzero(A\cap H)\), not by dividing by \(\muMzero(H)\). No limiting procedure, no normalization. It is simply conditional expectation applied to an indicator function. Concretely, in our example, let \(A=\{00,11\}\) (the event that both switches agree). Then \(\mathbf{1}_A(00)=1\), \(\mathbf{1}_A(01)=0\), \(\mathbf{1}_A(10)=0\), \(\mathbf{1}_A(11)=1\). On the group \(\{00,01\}\), the integral condition gives: \[\mathbb{P}(A\mid\mathcal{G}) ig|_{\{00,01\}}\cdot\muMzero(\{00,01\}) =\muMzero(A\cap\{00,01\})=\muMzero(\{00\})=0.4,\] so (since \(\muMzero(\{00,01\})=0.5>0\)): \[\mathbb{P}(A\mid\mathcal{G}) ig|_{\{00,01\}} = rac{0.4}{0.5}=0.8.\] On the group \(\{10,11\}\): \[\mathbb{P}(A\mid\mathcal{G}) ig|_{\{10,11\}}\cdot\muMzero(\{10,11\}) =\muMzero(A\cap\{10,11\})=\muMzero(\{11\})=0.4,\] so: \[\mathbb{P}(A\mid\mathcal{G}) ig|_{\{10,11\}} = rac{0.4}{0.5}=0.8.\] So \(\mathbb{P}(A\mid\mathcal{G})=0.8\) everywhere, knowing the first switch does not change the probability that both switches agree. The division by \(0.5\) here is a consequence of the integral definition in the discrete case, not the definition itself. In continuous spaces, where the group probability may be zero, the integral condition still works, no division is needed. Key idea Conditional probability is conditional expectation of an indicator function: \(\mathbb{P}(A\mid\mathcal{G})=\mathbb{E}[\mathbf{1}_A\mid\mathcal{G}]\), defined by the integral condition \(\int_H \mathbb{P}(A\mid\mathcal{G})\,d\mathbb{P}=\mathbb{P}(A\cap H)\). This is the bridge from the abstract notion of conditioning on a σ-algebra to the concrete probabilities we need for Bayesian inference. It works in discrete and continuous spaces alike, because it is built on integrals, not division. From σ-algebras to conditioning on a random variable So far we have conditioned on an abstract σ-algebra \(\mathcal{G}\). But in probability and Bayesian inference, information usually arrives through a random variable . In our switch problem, the observation is the reporter's output. Here we must be careful about where that random variable lives . It is tempting to write \(D:\M	o\D\), a function on the model space, but that would be wrong. For a fixed model state \(m\), the unreliable reporter can output either \(0\) or \(1\): the report is not a deterministic function of the model. A map defined on \(\M\) alone cannot carry reporting noise. The correct home for both the model and the report is an underlying sample space \(\Omega\) that describes the complete outcome, model state and report together. The simplest canonical choice is the product \[\Omega=\M	imes\D,\qquad \mathbb{P}=\Pi\ 	ext{(the joint measure from Part 2)},\] with the coordinate projections as random variables: \[M(m,d)=m,\qquad D(m,d)=d.\] Now \(D\) is a genuine random variable, and for a fixed \(m\) the pair \((m,0)\) and \((m,1)\) are distinct outcomes in \(\Omega\), the same model state with different reports, each carrying its own probability. If we observe \(D\), what have we learned? Not the full outcome \(\omega=(m,d)\); only its report coordinate. After observing \(D\), two outcomes \(\omega_1\) and \(\omega_2\) are indistinguishable whenever \[D(\omega_1)=D(\omega_2).\] So the information contained in \(D\) is exactly the information that can separate outcomes according to their data value, but no further. To turn this into a σ-algebra, take any data event \(B\subseteq\D\). For example, \(B=\{1\}\) asks: did the reporter output 1? This is a question in data space. To make it an event in the sample space, we pull it back through \(D\): \[D^{-1}(B)=\{\omega\in\Omega:D(\omega)\in B\}.\] This is the event: the outcome is one whose report lies in \(B\) . Preimages are the translation device from questions about data to events in the underlying probability space. F3c-2, Preimages translate data questions into sample-space events. The data lives in \(\D\), but probability is assigned to events in the sample space \(\Omega=\M	imes\D\). Preimages \(D^{-1}(B)\) are how data-questions become events in \(\Omega\). The σ-algebra generated by \(D\) is the collection of all such pullback events: \[\sigma(D)=\{D^{-1}(B):B\in\SigmaD\}.\] In words: \(\sigma(D)\) is the set of all events whose truth can be decided by looking only at \(D\). This σ-algebra partitions \(\Omega\) into the sets where \(D=0\) and where \(D=1\), the “indistinguishable classes” given the report. So conditioning on the random variable \(D\) means conditioning on the information it reveals: \[\mathbb{E}[X\mid D]:=\mathbb{E}[X\mid\sigma(D)].\] The same applies to probabilities. For an event \(A\), we define \[\mathbb{P}(A\mid D):=\mathbb{E}[\mathbf{1}_A\mid\sigma(D)].\] This object is \(\sigma(D)\)-measurable, so it is only allowed to depend on the outcome through the observed value of \(D\). If two outcomes carry the same report, then \(\mathbb{P}(A\mid D)\) must assign them the same value. Otherwise it would be using information that \(D\) does not contain. In other words, \(\sigma(D)\)-measurable means: cannot distinguish outcomes with the same \(D\)-value . Therefore there exists a function \(k_A:\D	o[0,1]\) such that \[\mathbb{P}(A\mid D)=k_A(D).\] This is the Doob–Dynkin lemma : any quantity measurable with respect to \(\sigma(D)\) can be written as a function of \(D\). In plain language: if a quantity only uses the information in \(D\), then it must be expressible as a function of the observed data value. Once the observed data value is \(\dobs\), the conditional probability is obtained by evaluation : \[\mathbb{P}(A\mid D=\dobs):=k_A(\dobs).\] The important point is that the conditioning was not performed by dividing by \(\mathbb{P}(D=\dobs)\). The conditioning was performed at the level of the σ-algebra \(\sigma(D)\). The final evaluation at \(\dobs\) comes only after the conditional probability has already been constructed as a function of the data. One honest caveat about that last step The function \(k_A\) is determined by the integral condition only \(\muD\)-almost everywhere : two versions of \(k_A\) may disagree on a set of data values with predictive probability zero, and both satisfy the definition. If \(\muD(\{\dobs\})>0\), as in our finite switch problem , the value \(k_A(\dobs)\) is pinned down and evaluation is harmless. But if \(\muD(\{\dobs\})=0\), the evaluation \(k_A(\dobs)\) depends on which version of \(k_A\) one uses. Conditioning on \(\sigma(D)\) removes the arbitrary geometry of the Borel–Kolmogorov limits, but it does not by itself single out a value at one specific null datum, for that, one needs a distinguished version of \(k_A\), typically supplied by extra regularity (a continuous density, a continuous disintegration, or an explicit likelihood formula). We will meet this again in Parts 3d and 3e. Key point The conditioning is performed at the level of the σ-algebra \(\sigma(D)\). Evaluating at \(\dobs\) is merely the final step. This is why the measure-theoretic approach handles continuous data spaces: the conditional distribution is defined by disintegration, not by dividing by the probability of a point. Conditioning on \(m_1=2\): BK ambiguity versus σ-algebra conditioning We now illustrate the power of the σ-algebra approach with a concrete example on \(\mathbb{R}^2\). This example revisits the Borel–Kolmogorov paradox from Part 3b , but now we have the tools to resolve it. Setup Let \((M_1,M_2)\) be a random vector on \(\mathbb{R}^2\) with a smooth joint density \(ho(m_1,m_2)\) with respect to Lebesgue measure, the standard notion of “volume” in \(\mathbb{R}^2\), which assigns to each rectangle its area and extends to more general sets. Saying that \(ho\) is a density with respect to Lebesgue measure simply means that probabilities are obtained by integrating \(ho\) over sets: \(\mathbb{P}((M_1,M_2)\in A)=\int_Aho\,dm_1\,dm_2\). Let \(A\subset\mathbb{R}^2\) be any measurable set. We wish to condition on \(m_1=2\). The set \(S:=\{(m_1,m_2)\in\mathbb{R}^2:m_1=2\}\) has probability zero, so naive conditioning on \(S\) is ill-defined. The BK constructions revisited In Part 3b, we saw two ways to “approach” the set \(S\) via limits. Let us now write down what those limits actually produce. Vertical strips. Define \(S^{(v)}_\varepsilon:=(2-\varepsilon,\,2+\varepsilon)	imes\mathbb{R}\) and the approximate conditional probability \(\mathbb{P}^{(v)}_\varepsilon(A):=\mathbb{P}(A\cap S^{(v)}_\varepsilon)/\mathbb{P}(S^{(v)}_\varepsilon)\). Suppose \(A\) is regular enough that the section integral \(m_1\mapsto\int_{\mathbb{R}}\mathbf{1}_A(m_1,m_2)\,ho(m_1,m_2)\,dm_2\) is continuous at \(m_1=2\), for instance, \(A\) open or closed with a well-behaved boundary. Then the limit exists and equals: \[\lim_{\varepsilon	o 0}\mathbb{P}^{(v)}_\varepsilon(A) = rac{\int_{\mathbb{R}}\mathbf{1}_A(2,m_2)\,ho(2,m_2)\,dm_2}{\int_{\mathbb{R}}ho(2,m_2)\,dm_2}.\] This defines a probability measure on the line \(m_1=2\) with density proportional to \(ho(2,m_2)\). The limit exists, but it already depends on a geometric choice: the use of vertical strips. Why the regularity assumption on \(A\)? For an arbitrary measurable \(A\), no such pointwise limit statement can hold at the specific point \(2\). The Lebesgue differentiation theorem guarantees the strip limit only at almost every base point, not at a point picked in advance. Worse: the vertical line \(\{2\}	imes\mathbb{R}\) has Lebesgue measure zero in the plane, so one can modify \(A\) on that line without changing any strip probability \(\mathbb{P}(A\cap S^{(v)}_\varepsilon)\), yet such a modification changes the section \(\mathbf{1}_A(2,\cdot)\) and hence the right-hand side. The limit of strip probabilities cannot possibly recover the literal section of every measurable representative. Regularity of \(A\) is what ties the section at \(2\) to the behaviour of \(A\) on nearby strips. Tilted tubes. Now define \(S^{(t)}_\varepsilon:=\{(m_1,m_2):|m_1-2| < \varepsilon|m_2|\}\) and the corresponding approximate conditional probability. A direct asymptotic expansion shows: \[\lim_{\varepsilon	o 0}\mathbb{P}^{(t)}_\varepsilon(A) = rac{\int_{\mathbb{R}}|m_2|\,\mathbf{1}_A(2,m_2)\,ho(2,m_2)\,dm_2}{\int_{\mathbb{R}}|m_2|\,ho(2,m_2)\,dm_2}.\] This is a different probability measure on the same line \(m_1=2\), obtained by weighting the density with \(|m_2|\). Both limits condition on the same measure-zero set, yet they produce different conditional distributions. This is the Borel–Kolmogorov paradox: the answer depends on the geometry of the limiting procedure, which probability theory does not specify. The resolution: conditioning via \(\sigma(M_1)\) Now let us do it properly. Define the data random variable \(D:=M_1\). This induces the σ-algebra: \[\sigma(D)=\sigma(M_1)=\{B	imes\mathbb{R}:B\in\mathcal{B}(\mathbb{R})\}.\] What are Borel sets? \(\mathcal{B}(\mathbb{R})\) is the collection of Borel sets , the sets you can build from intervals by taking countable unions, countable intersections, and complements. Think of them as “any set you might reasonably want to measure on the real line.” Intervals, open sets, closed sets, single points, and finite unions of intervals are all Borel sets. In practice, every set we encounter in probability is Borel, so you can read \(B\in\mathcal{B}(\mathbb{R})\) as “any reasonable subset of \(\mathbb{R}\).” F3c-3, The σ-algebra \(\sigma(M_1)\). Left: the model space \(\mathbb{R}^2\) with vertical strips \(B	imes\mathbb{R}\). Right: the corresponding Borel sets \(B\) on the real line (the \(m_1\)-axis). Each interval on \(\mathbb{R}\) lifts to a vertical strip in \(\mathbb{R}^2\); smaller intervals produce thinner strips. The σ-algebra \(\sigma(M_1)\) consists of all such strips, it encodes “we know \(m_1\) but not \(m_2\).” This σ-algebra encodes exactly the information “the value of \(m_1\) is observed.” For any measurable set \(A\), the conditional probability \(\mathbb{P}(A\mid\sigma(M_1))\) is defined via conditional expectation: \[\mathbb{P}(A\mid\sigma(M_1)):=\mathbb{E}[\mathbf{1}_A\mid\sigma(M_1)].\] By the Doob–Dynkin lemma, there exists a measurable function \(k_A:\mathbb{R}	o[0,1]\) such that \(\mathbb{P}(A\mid\sigma(M_1))(m_1,m_2)=k_A(m_1)\) almost everywhere. The defining identity of conditional expectation: \[\int_{B	imes\mathbb{R}} k_A(m_1)\,d\mathbb{P} =\mathbb{P}(A\cap(B	imes\mathbb{R}))\qquad orall\,B\in\mathcal{B}(\mathbb{R}),\] uniquely determines \(k_A\) (a.e.). A direct computation yields: \[k_A(m_1)= rac{\int_{\mathbb{R}}\mathbf{1}_A(m_1,m_2)\,ho(m_1,m_2)\,dm_2}{\int_{\mathbb{R}}ho(m_1,m_2)\,dm_2}\quad	ext{for a.e. }m_1.\] Note the qualifier “for a.e. \(m_1\)”: the integral identity determines \(k_A\) only up to sets of \(m_1\)-values with zero marginal probability, and the singleton \(\{2\}\) is such a set. So the identity alone does not force a value at \(m_1=2\). What we do instead is adopt the explicit ratio formula above as our chosen version of \(k_A\), it is a perfectly valid version, defined wherever the denominator \(\intho(m_1,m_2)\,dm_2\) is positive, and when \(ho\) is continuous it is the natural (continuous) representative. Evaluating this version at \(m_1=2\) gives: \[\mathbb{P}(A\mid M_1=2)=k_A(2)= rac{\int_{\mathbb{R}}\mathbf{1}_A(2,m_2)\,ho(2,m_2)\,dm_2}{\int_{\mathbb{R}}ho(2,m_2)\,dm_2}.\] This agrees with the vertical-strip limit. What the σ-algebra machinery has bought us is precisely this: the conditional is determined, with no geometric input , for almost every value of \(m_1\) simultaneously, and the joint density supplies a natural version whose value at \(2\) is the vertical-section formula. What it has not done, and what nothing can do, is force that value at the null point by conditional expectation alone. The honest statement is: σ-algebra conditioning eliminates the arbitrary tube geometry; the smooth density then singles out a canonical version; and the tilted-tube answer corresponds to conditioning a different observable, not to a different version of this one. But didn't you choose the vertical strips? A natural objection: “You ended up with the vertical-strip answer. So you secretly chose that geometry, you just wrapped it in fancy notation.” Not quite. The vertical strips are not chosen because they look nice. They are chosen because they are the sets of indistinguishability of the observation \(D=M_1\). Two points \((m_1,m_2)\) and \((m_1',m_2')\) with \(m_1=m_1'\) produce the same observed value, the observation cannot tell them apart. The σ-algebra \(\sigma(M_1)\) is built from exactly these indistinguishable classes: vertical strips \(B	imes\mathbb{R}\). If the observation were different, say \(D=M_1\cdot M_2\) , the indistinguishable classes would be hyperbolic curves (for nonzero observed values; the level set of \(0\) is the union of the two coordinate axes), not vertical strips. The σ-algebra \(\sigma(D)\) would reflect that geometry automatically. No manual choice is made; the observation mapping determines the σ-algebra, and the σ-algebra determines the conditioning. In other words: the geometry is not chosen for aesthetic reasons , it is imposed by what the observation can and cannot distinguish. This is what makes σ-algebra conditioning canonical . But what about the σ-algebra on the data space? A deeper objection: “Fine, the σ-algebra \(\sigma(D)\) on the model space is determined by \(D\). But \(\sigma(D)\) is built from preimages of sets in the data space \(\D\). How do you choose the σ-algebra on \(\D\)? The final answer depends on that choice too! So your answer is still not canonical.” This is a fair point, and the answer is that the σ-algebra on the data space does matter, but its choice is rooted in the physics of the problem and the coarseness of observations . Can the measurement distinguish two data values that differ by arbitrarily small difference? Then singletons are effectively distinguishable, and we use the finest σ-algebra (the Borel one). Or perhaps the instrument bins its output: all values in \([0,0.1)\) are reported as “0”, all in \([0.1,0.2)\) as “1”, and so on. Then the data σ-algebra is coarser , it contains the bins, not individual points, and the induced \(\sigma(D)\) on the model space reflects that coarseness. In each case, the choice is defensible and canonical for the physical situation . It is not arbitrary, it encodes what the instrument can actually resolve. (For a binned instrument, an equivalent and often cleaner modelling choice is to define the reported variable itself as the bin label, \(D_{\mathrm{bin}}=Q(D)\) for a quantization map \(Q\), and give the finite set of labels its full power-set σ-algebra.) In practice, we most often assume that observations can be distinguished down to singletons (individual points in the data space), so we equip \(\D\) with the Borel σ-algebra, the standard σ-algebra generated by the topology, i.e. the smallest σ-algebra containing all open sets. (It is not the finest σ-algebra on the space, the Lebesgue σ-algebra and the full power set are strictly finer, but it is the standard choice, and it makes every open-set question measurable.) This is the convention we adopt throughout this essay. To be explicit: we are dealing with two σ-algebras here. The first is the one we choose on the data space \(\D\), based on the physics of what the instrument can resolve. The second is \(\sigma(D)\) on the model space, which is automatically determined by the random variable \(D\), we do not choose it, the observation mapping constructs it for us. And \(D\) itself is chosen by us based on physical arguments: it encodes how the model produces the data. So the chain is: physics determines the data σ-algebra and the observation mapping; the observation mapping determines \(\sigma(D)\); \(\sigma(D)\) determines the conditioning. Everything is rooted in the reality of the problem, and canonical with respect to that reality. Key point The two BK constructions differ because they introduce additional geometric structure (tube shapes) that is not specified by the observation model. Conditioning via the σ-algebra \(\sigma(M_1)\) is canonical up to null sets : it is completely determined, for almost every observed value, by what the data mapping \(D=M_1\) can distinguish and the joint distribution. BK ambiguity arises when conditioning on measure-zero sets via ad hoc geometric limits. What remains at a single null datum is not geometric ambiguity but version ambiguity, and a smooth joint density resolves it by supplying a natural continuous version. References Andrey N. Kolmogorov (1933). Grundbegriffe der Wahrscheinlichkeitsrechnung . Springer. (In English: Foundations of the Theory of Probability .) Conditional expectation via the Radon–Nikodym theorem: Kolmogorov’s construction. doi:10.1007/978-3-642-49888-6 Olav Kallenberg (2021). Foundations of Modern Probability , 3rd edition. Springer. The standard modern development of conditional expectation. doi:10.1007/978-3-030-61871-1 ← Part 3b: Conditioning and Its Limits Part 3d: Disintegration →

Act 6: The Best Possible Descent Optimality, the polynomial picture, and life in infinite dimensions. Part of The Road to Conjugate Gradients . « Overview 1 The Landscape 2 Rolling Downhill 3 The Right Geometry 4 Never Undo Work 5 Conjugate Gradients 6 The Best Descent Optimal, Not Merely Good Act 5 built CG from one particular choice, residuals as raw material, and it is fair to be suspicious of particular choices. Could a different construction, perhaps with look-ahead, or randomization, or some cleverness not yet invented, do better with the same resources? Formulate the question honestly. Any method that starts at \(u_0\) and may only apply \(A\), add, scale, and take inner products has, after \(k\) operator applications, an iterate confined to the reachable set of Act 5: \[	ilde u_k \in u_0 + \Kry_k.\] That is the level playing field: every competitor, however clever, produces a point of the same affine subspace. Now recall what CG achieves. The expanding-subspace theorem (Act 4) plus the span identity (Act 5) say that the CG iterate minimizes \(J\) over precisely \(u_0 + \Kry_k\); and the error identity (Act 3) converts minimal \(J\) into minimal energy-norm error. Chain the three, each act contributing exactly one link, and: Theorem: Krylov optimality of CG \[
ormA{u_k^{\mathrm{CG}} - u} \;=\; \min_{v \,\in\, u_0 + \Kry_k} 
ormA{v - u}.\] At every step, the CG iterate is the best approximation to the solution that any black-box method could have produced from the same information, measured in the energy norm. The greedy-looking recurrence secretly performs global optimization over everything it has ever learned. (The minimization properties go back to §4 of [HS52] ; the statement in this Krylov form is Lemma 6.28 in Saad [Saa03] , §6.11.3.) This is why the question “could something do better?” has a clean answer: not in this norm, not with this information. The search for a better method is over before it starts; the only remaining freedoms are to change the information (preconditioning, below) or the norm (other Krylov methods, other trade-offs). The Polynomial Picture Optimality also hands us, almost by accident, the sharpest available lens on how fast CG converges. Elements of \(\Kry_k\) are polynomials in \(A\) applied to \(r_0\) (the popup of Act 5), and \(r_0 = A(u - u_0)\), so a short manipulation turns the optimality statement into a statement about polynomials: writing \(e_0 = u_0 - u\), \[
ormA{u_k - u} \;=\; \min_{\substack{\pi 	ext{ poly of degree} \le k \ \pi(0) = 1}} \;
ormA{\pi(A)\, e_0}.\] The method is, in effect, free to apply to its initial error any degree-\(k\) polynomial of the operator with \(\pi(0)=1\), and it automatically applies the best one. Why is that powerful? Because \(\pi(A)\) acts on each spectral component of the error as multiplication by \(\pi(\lambda)\), \(\lambda\) running over the spectrum of \(A\). So \[
ormA{u_k - u} \;\le\; \Bigl(\max_{\lambda \in \operatorname{spec}(A)} |\pi(\lambda)|\Bigr)\, 
ormA{e_0} \qquad	ext{for any admissible } \pi,\] and convergence analysis becomes a question you can draw: how small can a degree-\(k\) polynomial with \(\pi(0)=1\) be on the spectrum? If all we know is that the spectrum lies in an interval \([\gamma, \Gamma]\), the best choice is a scaled Chebyshev polynomial, and the classical answer is \[
ormA{u_k - u} \;\le\; 2\left( rac{\sqrt{\kappa}-1}{\sqrt{\kappa}+1}ight)^{k} 
ormA{e_0}, \qquad \kappa = \Gamma/\gamma.\] There it is: the celebrated \(\sqrt{\kappa}\). Where gradient descent contracts like \((\kappa-1)/(\kappa+1)\), CG contracts like the same expression with \(\kappa\) replaced by its square root, the difference between 11,500 steps per digit and about 58 at \(\kappa = 10^4\). (The Chebyshev argument is worked out in Saad [Saa03] , §6.11.3, Theorem 6.29; the same bound in the Hilbert-space setting of these notes is due to Daniel [Dan67] .) Figure FC6-1. The \(\kappa\)-vs-\(\sqrt{\kappa}\) gap. Left: error factor per step. Right: the same information as “steps needed for six digits”, note both axes are logarithmic; at \(\kappa=10^4\) the gap is a factor of about 200. The spectrum is the whole story But the Chebyshev bound is only the pessimist’s corner of the polynomial picture, because it uses nothing about the spectrum except its endpoints. The minimizing polynomial sees everything. Suppose the eigenvalues gather in \(m\) tight clusters: a degree-\(m\) polynomial can place one root in each cluster and be tiny on the entire spectrum, so CG essentially converges in \(m\) steps, regardless of \(\kappa\). The refined theory , including the famous superlinear acceleration of CG as the extreme eigenvalues are progressively “solved off”, is due to van der Sluis and van der Vorst [SV86] . Same \(\kappa\), wildly different behaviour: Figure FC6-2. Two 60-dimensional problems with the same \(\kappa = 90\). Three tight clusters (drawn here in their limiting case, three exactly repeated eigenvalues): machine precision in exactly three steps, one root of the minimizing polynomial per cluster. Eigenvalues spread evenly: the Chebyshev regime, dozens of steps. \(\kappa\) alone never told the whole story. Remark: why preconditioning is the real game This picture explains the word you will meet everywhere in practice: preconditioning . If we can cheaply apply an operator \(M^{-1}\approx A^{-1}\), then solving \(M^{-1}Au = M^{-1}f\) (symmetrized appropriately) runs CG on an operator whose spectrum is compressed and clustered near 1. Nothing about the CG iteration changes , we change the geometry it runs in , exactly in the spirit of Act 3: a good preconditioner is a change of inner product that makes the bowl rounder. Designing \(M\) is problem-specific artistry, and it is where the practical battles are won, Saad [Saa03] devotes two full chapters (9–10) to it. Infinite Dimensions, Honestly Time to collect a debt. From Act 1 onward we have insisted on working in a Hilbert space, promising the effort would pay. Here is the audit of what is, and is not, true when \(\dim\H = \infty\). What survives untouched. Every derivation in Acts 1–5, the master identity, the line search, conjugacy, the expanding-subspace theorem, the short recurrence, Krylov optimality, the Chebyshev bound. None of them ever mentioned a basis or a dimension. If the spectrum of \(A\) lies in \([\gamma,\Gamma]\) with \(\gamma > 0\) (our standing positive-definiteness), CG converges linearly in \(\H\) at the \(\sqrt{\kappa}\) rate, full stop, this is precisely the setting of Daniel’s 1967 operator-equation treatment [Dan67] . What is lost. Exactly one thing: finite termination. The \(n\)-step guarantee of Act 4 is meaningless without an \(n\). In infinite dimensions CG is honestly an iterative method, run until the residual is small enough, like everything else. Given that the \(n\)-step property was never usable in practice anyway (rounding destroys it, and \(n\) is astronomically large), the loss is philosophical rather than practical. What discretization means. To compute, one picks a finite-dimensional subspace \(V_h \subset \H\) (finite elements, spectral modes, …) and solves the Galerkin-projected problem: find \(u_h \in V_h\) with \(\ip{Au_h}{v_h} = \ip{f}{v_h}\) for all \(v_h \in V_h\). Two facts make this beautiful rather than merely necessary. First, the projected operator inherits self-adjointness and positive definiteness, so the entire theory applies verbatim on \(V_h\). Second, readers of Think First, Discretize Later will recognize that the method, its geometry, and its convergence theory were fixed before the mesh appeared. The discretization samples the problem; it does not define it. If your discrete CG behaves qualitatively differently under mesh refinement, that is a signal about your formulation, not about CG. Warning: the honest boundary Positive definiteness with \(\gamma>0\) is a real restriction. The integral operators of inverse theory, the compact, smoothing \(G^*G\) of tomography, for instance, have spectra accumulating at zero: \(\gamma = 0\), \(\kappa = \infty\). CG can still be run, and each iterate is still Krylov-optimal, but there is no rate, and iterates eventually amplify data noise rather than converge, iteration count itself becomes a regularization parameter. This regime has its own careful theory: Hanke’s monograph [Han95] is devoted to CG-type methods for ill-posed problems, and Engl, Hanke & Neubauer [EHN96] place them within regularization theory at large. At that boundary, the solver question turns into an inference question: what do we actually want from an ill-posed problem? That is where these notes hand over to My Take on SOLA and the Bayesian series . The Road, in Hindsight Walk it backwards once, and notice that at no point did we have a real choice: The black box permits only iteration (Act 1) the equation is a bowl, and the residual points downhill (Act 1) greedy descent is exact on a line, and its line searches force Euclidean right angles, the staircase (Act 2) “never spoil finished work” is the equation \(\ipA{q}{p}=0\): the landscape’s own geometry (Act 3) conjugate line searches make progress permanent and minimize over expanding subspaces (Act 4) the only raw material is the residual stream, and Krylov structure collapses its orthogonalization to two terms (Act 5) the result is optimal among all methods with the same information (Act 6) . The slogan The conjugate gradient method is not an invention to be memorized. It is what remains of gradient descent after every mistake is repaired in the only way the geometry allows, the last method standing when all arbitrary choices are removed. If you now open any textbook’s CG pseudocode, or the algorithm box of Act 5 , you should find that no line of it could have been otherwise: \(\alpha_k\) is a parabola bottoming out, \( eta_k\) is Gram–Schmidt with its corpses removed, and the whole loop is a machine for keeping promises made to previous directions. That was the promise of these notes; whether it was kept is for you to judge, ideally with the race panel still open in another tab. References Magnus R. Hestenes and Eduard Stiefel (1952). “Methods of Conjugate Gradients for Solving Linear Systems.” Journal of Research of the National Bureau of Standards 49(6), 409–436. Minimization properties: §4. doi:10.6028/jres.049.044 · free PDF (NIST) James W. Daniel (1967). “The Conjugate Gradient Method for Linear and Nonlinear Operator Equations.” SIAM Journal on Numerical Analysis 4(1), 10–26. CG and its \(\sqrt{\kappa}\) bound for bounded s.p.d. operators on Hilbert space. doi:10.1137/0704002 Yousef Saad (2003). Iterative Methods for Sparse Linear Systems , 2nd edition. SIAM. Convergence analysis: §6.11.3 (Lemma 6.28, Theorem 6.29); preconditioning: Chs. 9–10. doi:10.1137/1.9780898718003 · free PDF (author’s edition) A. van der Sluis and H. A. van der Vorst (1986). “The rate of convergence of Conjugate Gradients.” Numerische Mathematik 48, 543–560. doi:10.1007/BF01389450 · free scan (EuDML) Martin Hanke (1995). Conjugate Gradient Type Methods for Ill-Posed Problems . Longman / Chapman & Hall–CRC. doi:10.1201/9781315140193 Heinz W. Engl, Martin Hanke, and Andreas Neubauer (1996). Regularization of Inverse Problems . Kluwer. publisher page (Springer) ← Act 5: The Conjugate Gradient Method Back to the Overview →
